bbb-opts opt_elim_dead_assign (-fbbb=e): wrong code, ext.l of a shift count dropped.

The extension of a register used as a shift count was deleted as superfluous
without checking that the register is dead behind the shift, and also across
jumps. "int n = *short_ptr; return (x << n) + n;" gave
    move.w (a0),d1 / lsl.l d1,d0 / add.l d1,d0
so add.l read a register whose high word was never set. -O2 and -Os, every CPU.
The scan now stops at jumps and the extension only goes if the register is dead.

--- a/gcc/bbb-opts.c
+++ b/gcc/bbb-opts.c
@@ -4790,13 +4790,18 @@
 	      for (int jndex = index - 1; jndex > 0; --jndex)
 		{
 		  insn_info & jj = (*infos)[jndex];
-		  if (jj.is_label())
+		  /* Stay inside the block: past a jump the other path may read
+		     the extended value.  */
+		  if (jj.is_label() || jj.is_jump())
 		    break;
 
 		  // ZERO_EXTEND and SIGN_EXTEND use and def that register.
 		  if (jj.is_myuse(opreg))
 		    {
-		      if (jj.get_dst_regno() == opreg &&
+		      /* The shift reads only the low bits of the count, whatever
+		         comes after it may read all of them: the extension is only
+		         superfluous if the register is dead behind the shift.  */
+		      if (jj.get_dst_regno() == opreg && is_reg_dead (opreg, index) &&
 			  (jj.get_src_op() == ZERO_EXTEND || jj.get_src_op() == SIGN_EXTEND))
 			{
 			  delete_and_update(jndex);
